<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byrsa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Byrsa is the hill at the centre of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, on which the city's citadel stood. According to legend, Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a> was granted as much land as an ox-hide could cover and cut the hide into thin strips to encircle the hill, the name being linked to the Greek word for hide. Byrsa was the heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>'s capital and the last stronghold to fall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 146 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byrsa.jpg" alt="Byrsa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pradigue · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maison_punique_byrsa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
